>> title: Lo Mein Framework
>> description: An adaptable noodle stir-fry built from a starch base, protein, vegetables, aromatics, sauce, and garnish.
>> servings: 4
>> prep time: 15 minutes
>> cook time: 15 minutes
>> cuisine: Chinese-American
>> course: dinner
>> tags: lo mein, noodles, stir-fry, flexible, weeknight
>> author: Ethan Chlebowski
>> source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eoRrxpErI0o

1. Bring a large pot of salted water to a boil. Cook @lo mein noodles{1%lb} according to the package directions until just tender. Drain, rinse briefly with warm water to loosen, and set aside.

2. Whisk together @soy sauce{4%tbsp}, @rice vinegar{2%tbsp}, and @honey{1%tbsp}. This is the 2:1:0.5 salty-to-sour-to-sweet sauce ratio; add @toasted sesame oil{1%tsp}, @garlic{2%cloves}, @ginger{1%tbsp}, and @red pepper flakes{0.25%tsp} to taste.

3. Thinly slice @protein of choice{12%oz} and prepare @vegetables of choice{4%cups} in bite-sized pieces. Good choices include chicken, pork, beef, shrimp, or tofu for the protein and cabbage, broccoli, peppers, carrots, mushrooms, snow peas, or bean sprouts for the vegetables.

4. Heat a wok or large skillet over high heat. Add half of the @neutral oil{2%tbsp}, then stir-fry the @protein of choice{12%oz} until browned and cooked through. Transfer it to a plate.

5. Add the remaining @neutral oil{2%tbsp} to the wok. Stir-fry the @vegetables of choice{4%cups} until brightly colored and crisp-tender, then transfer them to the plate with the protein.

6. Pour the sauce into the hot wok and cook for about 30 seconds until fragrant. Return the @protein of choice{12%oz} and @vegetables of choice{4%cups} to the wok with the @lo mein noodles{1%lb}.

7. Toss everything over high heat for 1 to 2 minutes until the noodles are glossy and evenly coated. If the noodles stick or the sauce is too thick, loosen with a splash of hot water. Taste and adjust with more soy sauce, rice vinegar, or honey as needed.

8. Divide among bowls and garnish with sliced @scallions{2} and any additional sesame oil or chili you like.

-- Framework: starch base + protein + vegetables + aromatics + sauce + garnish.
-- Use whatever noodles you have, including spaghetti, udon, or soba. Batch-cooked noodles work especially well.
-- The protein and vegetables are intentionally interchangeable; cook dense vegetables first and delicate vegetables last.
